\subsection{Selecting the events to show}

The count is a slice length, so its two out-of-band values need translating
before we can slice with it.
Zero is the documented \enquote{show everything} spelling, which we turn
into the length of the match list.
A negative count is nonsense we cannot honour, and handing it to the slice
would be worse than clamping it: Python counts a negative bound from the
end, so $-1$ would show every match \emph{but} the last, and anything below
minus the number of matches would slice to nothing---which the empty check
below would then report as \enquote{no matching events}, blaming the
calendar for what was a bad argument.
Clamping to a single match keeps the outcome legible.

<<let [[selected]] be the first [[count]] events>>=
if args.count == 0:
  count = len(filtered)
else:
  count = max(1, args.count)

selected = filtered[:count]
@

\subsection{Saying what the count hid}

The count defaults to one because [[calendar view]] is meant for reading a
single event in detail; listing many is what [[calendar list]] is for.
Truncation is therefore the \emph{normal} case rather than an exceptional
one---and that is precisely why it must be announced.
A range query such as
\begin{center}
  [[canvaslms calendar view --start-time 2026-03-09 --end-time 2026-03-14]]
\end{center}
looks like a query for a week's events, but prints the first match and
nothing else.
Without a word about the other matches, a single event is indistinguishable
from a week that really did hold one event, and the reader concludes the
fetch is broken rather than the count too small.

The notice goes to standard error, never to standard output.
Standard output carries either the labelled lines or the JSON array, and a
consumer piping [[--json]] into [[jq]] would choke on a human sentence
mixed into the stream; diagnostics are exactly what standard error is for.
We say it with [[canvaslms.cli.warn]] rather than a bare
[[print(..., file=sys.stderr)]], because [[warn]] routes through the
logging setup that [[cli.nw]] configures: the message lands on standard
error, and the [[-q]] flag already means \enquote{spare me the warnings},
so a script that wants pure output has a way to ask for it.

Truncation is a property of the selection, not of the output format---both
branches above lose the same events---so a single notice after them covers
both and cannot drift apart the way two copies would.
Putting it after the events, rather than before, also leaves it as the last
line on the terminal, where a reader who has just scrolled past a screenful
of output will still see it.

<<warn about matches hidden by [[--count]]>>=
if len(selected) < len(filtered):
  canvaslms.cli.warn(
    f"showing {len(selected)} of {len(filtered)} matching events; "
    f"use --count 0 to show all")
@

\subsection{Machine-readable output}

The details we print by default are laid out for a human reader: labelled
lines, a wrapped description, one line per booked participant.  A script
that only wants, say, the URL of every matching event would have to parse
those labels back out again---and any change to the layout would break it.
So we also offer the events as JSON.

<<add calendar view arguments>>=
calendar_view_parser.add_argument("--json",
  action="store_true", default=False,
  help="Output matching events as JSON")
@

What should that JSON contain?  A canvasapi [[CalendarEvent]] is little
more than the JSON object Canvas sent us: [[set_attributes]] turns each
key of the API response into an instance attribute.  Serialising the
object's attribute dictionary therefore hands the consumer back
essentially the event as Canvas described it, ready for [[jq]] or
[[json.loads]].  The alternative we used to take---printing
[[repr(selected)]]---produced a Python list of object reprs, which no
JSON parser accepts, so the [[--json]] flag promised something it never
delivered.

Two properties of that attribute dictionary need care.
The attributes beginning with an underscore are canvasapi's own
bookkeeping rather than data about the event; [[_requester]], which holds
the HTTP session, is not even serialisable.  We drop them.
Timestamps get a derived [[datetime]] twin ([[start_at_date]] alongside
[[start_at]], and so on) that [[json.dumps]] likewise cannot handle on
its own, so we render those with [[default=str]].
Note that this is a weaker conversion than the one [[format_json]]
applies to assignments, where every value is passed through [[str]]:
calendar events carry nested structures---the [[child_events]] of an
appointment group, for instance---and stringifying those would flatten
whole booking lists into single Python reprs.  With [[default=str]] only
the values JSON cannot express are converted, and the nesting survives.

<<functions>>=
def format_events_json(events):
  """Returns a JSON representation of a list of calendar events"""
  return json.dumps([
    {key: value for key, value in event.__dict__.items()
      if not key.startswith("_")}
    for event in events
  ], indent=2, default=str)
@

The human-readable form is the counterpart: one labelled line per piece
of information, expanding an appointment group into the students who
booked its slots.
