\section{Verifying the truncation notice}

Both halves of the notice are easy to lose in a later edit: someone tuning
the wording could route it to standard output, and someone reworking the
selection could stop emitting it altogether.
Neither mistake announces itself---a truncated run looks exactly like an
honest one---so we pin the notice down with tests instead.
The test file itself, with its imports and the fixtures it draws on, is
introduced further down in this chapter; the chunks below add to it.

The command reads its arguments straight off [[args]], so, as for
[[calendar create]], we build the namespace that [[argparse]] would have
produced and let each test override only what it varies.

<<test helper functions>>=
def view_args(**overrides):
    """Build the args namespace that `calendar view` would receive"""
    args = argparse.Namespace(
        on=None,
        start_time="2026-03-09",
        end_time="2026-03-14",
        course=None,
        match=None,
        title=None,
        description=None,
        ignore_case=False,
        count=1,
        json=False,
    )
    for name, value in overrides.items():
        setattr(args, name, value)
    return args

@

The events themselves are plain attribute bags rather than mocks.
A [[MagicMock]] answers every attribute lookup, so
[[get_appointment_booking_data]] would see booking metadata on every event
and the printer would try to walk a mock's [[child_events]]; a namespace
answers only what we put in it, which is what [[getattr(event, ..., None)]]
expects of a real event that is not an appointment group.
Serialising to JSON needs nothing more either, since
[[format_events_json]] works from [[__dict__]].
We give the events distinct start times so the sort is deterministic and
the first match is predictable.

<<test helper functions>>=
import json
import types


def calendar_events(count):
    """Build `count` events, an hour apart, as Canvas would return them"""
    return [
        types.SimpleNamespace(
            title=f"Event {number}",
            description=f"Description {number}",
            start_at=f"2026-03-09T{9 + number:02d}:00:00Z",
            end_at=f"2026-03-09T{10 + number:02d}:00:00Z",
            location_name="Room",
            html_url=f"https://canvas.example.com/events/{number}",
        )
        for number in range(count)
    ]

