\section{The \texttt{calendar create} subcommand}

The \texttt{calendar create} subcommand creates a new calendar event.
It supports two types of events:
\begin{itemize}
\item Regular calendar events (\texttt{--event-type event}, default)
\item Appointment groups for bookable time slots ( [[appointment_group]] )
\end{itemize}

Appointment groups allow students to book time slots within a defined time range.
This is useful for office hours, lab sessions, or individual consultations.
<<functions>>=
def add_calendar_create_command(subp):
  """Adds the calendar create subcommand and its options to argparse subparser subp"""  
  calendar_create_parser = subp.add_parser("create",
    help="Creates a new calendar event", 
    description="Creates a new calendar event or appointment group for bookable time slots")
  calendar_create_parser.set_defaults(func=calendar_create_command)
  courses.add_course_option(calendar_create_parser, default=None)
  <<add calendar create arguments>>

def calendar_create_command(config, canvas, args):
  """Creates a new calendar event"""
  <<create calendar event>>
@

The create command requires only a title and a start time; everything else is
optional.
In particular, Canvas happily stores an event that has no end time at all---an
open-ended entry such as a deadline or a reminder, which the calendar renders
as a point in time rather than as an interval.
We therefore declare [[--end-time]] without [[required=True]], which makes
\begin{quote}
[[canvaslms calendar create "Assignment 3 due" --start-time "2026-08-15 17:00"]]
\end{quote}
a complete command line.

Appointment groups are the exception: they subdivide an interval into bookable
slots, so they need both bounds.
Making [[--end-time]] mandatory for every event type would be the easy way to
guarantee that, but it would also take open-ended events away from everyone to
serve one event type.
We instead let the appointment-group code demand an end time where it needs
one, when it computes the slots below.
<<add calendar create arguments>>=
calendar_create_parser.add_argument("title",
  help="Title of the calendar event")
calendar_create_parser.add_argument("--start-time", required=True,
  help="Start time (ISO format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm)")
calendar_create_parser.add_argument("--end-time",
  help="End time (ISO format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm)")
calendar_create_parser.add_argument("--description", 
  help="Description of the event")
calendar_create_parser.add_argument("--location",
  help="Location of the event")
calendar_create_parser.add_argument("--event-type", 
  choices=["event", "appointment_group"],
  default="event",
  help="Type of calendar event to create (default: event)")
calendar_create_parser.add_argument("--time-slot-duration", type=int,
  help="Duration in minutes for each bookable time slot (only for appointment_group type)")
calendar_create_parser.add_argument("--max-appointments", type=int, default=1,
  help="Maximum appointments per time slot (only for appointment_group type, default: 1)")
@

Creating an event takes three steps: we turn the user's date and time strings
into instants, we sanity-check them against each other, and only then do we
call the API for the requested event type, which is where the instants are
finally written out as Canvas timestamps.
<<create calendar event>>=
<<parse start and end times>>
<<reject events that end before they start>>

try:
  if args.event_type == "appointment_group":
    <<create appointment group>>
  else:
    <<create regular calendar event>>
except canvasapi.exceptions.CanvasException as e:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, f"Failed to create calendar event: {e}")
@

We interpret the start and end times ([[args.start_time]] and
[[args.end_time]]) as local time in the system timezone, resolved through
[[utils.local_timezone()]].
The predecessor here was [[arrow]]'s own [[tzinfo='local']], which reads like
the local zone and is a single offset sampled when the command runs
(\cref{sec:utils:local-zone}).
Scheduling ahead is where the two part company: entered from a Stockholm
terminal in July, [[--start-time '2023-12-15 09:00']] was stamped with CEST
and reached Canvas as [[07:00Z]] instead of [[08:00Z]], putting the meeting an
hour before the one the user typed---and every timestamp in the exchange looks
perfectly ordinary, so nothing in the output says so.

The start time is always there, since [[--start-time]] is required, but the end
time is [[None]] whenever the user omitted [[--end-time]]---and [[arrow.get]]
does not parse [[None]], it raises a [[TypeError]] instead.
We must therefore ask whether we have an end time \emph{before} we try to parse
one; the conditional expression lets a missing end time travel onwards as
[[end_time = None]] rather than as an exception.

Guarding the parse is what makes the absent end time visible at all.
The [[except]] clause is deliberately broad, because [[arrow]] reports every
malformed input differently, but that breadth means it cannot distinguish a
typo from a missing value: parsing [[None]] unconditionally reported
\enquote{no end time given} as the fatal and misleading
\enquote{Invalid date/time format: Cannot parse argument of type None},
which made open-ended events impossible to create at all.

What we keep is the parsed instant, not its printed form.
Everything the command does with these values before it calls Canvas asks a
question about \emph{time}---is the end after the start, how many slots fit
between them---and only the request we finally hand to [[canvasapi]] asks for
text.
Formatting here would satisfy the last of those steps and leave the earlier
ones to either reparse the string or reason about how it was spelled; keeping
[[arrow]] objects until the API boundary lets them compare and add instants
instead.
<<parse start and end times>>=
try:
  start_time = arrow.get(args.start_time, tzinfo=utils.local_timezone())
  end_time = arrow.get(
    args.end_time, tzinfo=utils.local_timezone()) if args.end_time else None
except Exception as e:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, f"Invalid date/time format: {e} "
                       f"(did you write 8:15 instead of 08:15?)")
@

The check is a comparison, and it matters a great deal \emph{what} we compare.
Formatted timestamps invite a shortcut: both come out of
[[format_canvas_time]] as UTC ISO text ending in [[Z]], and for two strings of
the same shape the alphabetical order and the chronological one agree.
That coincidence is the trap.
It holds only while every timestamp is spelled identically, and [[isoformat]]
does not spell them identically---it prints fractional seconds only when there
are any.
An event starting at [[10:00:00]] and ending half a second later thus yields
[[...T10:00:00Z]] and [[...T10:00:00.500000Z]], where the [[.]] sorts before
the [[Z]]: alphabetically the end precedes the start, and the command rejects
an event that is perfectly well formed.
Swap the two times and the same rule accepts an event that really does end
before it starts.

Comparing the [[arrow]] instants removes the question rather than answering it.
[[arrow]] orders by the moment a value denotes, not by how it is written, so
the check stops depending on how many digits [[isoformat]] chose to print---or
on both bounds having been printed by the same function at all.
An event that never ends cannot end too early, so the ordering check applies
only when the user actually gave us an end time.
<<reject events that end before they start>>=
if end_time and end_time < start_time:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, "End time cannot be before start time.")
@

\subsection{Distinguishing no course from all courses}

Both event types must know which course contexts the new event belongs
to, and both learn it from the [[-c]] option added by
[[courses.add_course_option]].
For the listing commands that option defaults to the match-all regex
[[.*]] (\cref{sec:calendar:course-default}), and for them the default is
harmless: reading every calendar and reading with no filter are the same
request.

Creating is not so forgiving.
[[calendar create]] used to share that default and test [[args.course]]
for truthiness only; since the default regex matches every course the
user is enrolled in,
\begin{verbatim}
canvaslms calendar create "Meeting" --start-time "2026-08-01 10:00" \
                                    --end-time "2026-08-01 11:00"
\end{verbatim}
looped over all of them and created one copy of \enquote{Meeting} in
each---silently, with no confirmation, and with no bulk delete to undo
it.
Nor can the command recover by comparing [[args.course]] against the
default regex: an explicit [[-c '.*']]---a user who really does want the
event in every course---produces exactly the same [[args]] as no [[-c]]
at all, so the comparison silently takes that ability away.

The distinction the command needs can only live in the default itself.
[[calendar create]] therefore registers its course option with
[[default=None]]: a [[None]] means the user never mentioned courses, and
any string---the match-all [[.*]] included---is an explicit request that
we honour by creating the event in every matching course.
Both branches below test [[args.course]] against [[None]] by truthiness,
which is now sound because argparse never turns an absent [[-c]] into a
non-empty string.

\subsection{Regular calendar events}

We first look at how we create a regular calendar event.
A Canvas calendar event lives in exactly one context, so an explicit
[[-c]] regex matching several courses means several events: we set the
shared data once and then create \emph{one} event for \emph{every}
matched course.
When no course was selected we still must name a context: Canvas
refuses an event without one (\enquote{Dictionary with key
'[[context_code]]' is required}), so there is no such thing as omitting the
context and letting the server pick.
The user's own calendar \emph{is} a context of its own, addressed as
[[user_<id>]], so we look up the authenticated user and file the event
there---the natural reading of a [[calendar create]] that never
mentions a course.

Only the title and the start time go into [[params]] unconditionally; each
optional field is added only when the user supplied it.
That is what carries an open-ended event through to Canvas: we say
\enquote{this event has no end} by leaving [[end_at]] out of the request
altogether, rather than by sending a key whose value is [[None]].
The parse above and this assembly thus agree---when [[args.end_time]] is
missing, [[end_time]] stays [[None]] and never reaches the request.

This is also where the instants become text.
[[params]] is what leaves for Canvas, so the entries we put into it are the
last values we can still reason about as times, and applying
[[format_canvas_time]] as they enter is what keeps the conversion at that
boundary instead of ahead of it.
<<create regular calendar event>>=
params = {
  'calendar_event': {
    'title': args.title,
    'start_at': utils.format_canvas_time(start_time),
  }
}

if args.end_time:
  params['calendar_event']['end_at'] = utils.format_canvas_time(end_time)
if args.description:
  params['calendar_event']['description'] = args.description  
if args.location:
  params['calendar_event']['location_name'] = args.location
  
if args.course:
  course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)
  for course in course_list:
    params['calendar_event']['context_code'] = f"course_{course.id}"
    event = canvas.create_calendar_event(**params)
else:
  user = canvas.get_current_user()
  params['calendar_event']['context_code'] = f"user_{user.id}"
  event = canvas.create_calendar_event(**params)
@

\subsection{Appointment groups}

Appointment groups (bookable timeslots) , on the other hand, are created
differently.
Here we can create one appointment group for many course contexts---thus having
one appointment group joint for several courses.

The user's own calendar is no fallback here.
An appointment group exists so that participants can book its slots, and
Canvas accordingly requires at least one context code: without a course
there is no set of students who could book anything.
That leaves us two ways to react to a missing [[-c]], guess or stop.
Guessing means every course the user is enrolled in---exactly the
accident we set out to prevent, only now the user has also published
bookable office hours to strangers.
So we stop, and we name the option the user has to supply.
<<create appointment group>>=
params = {
  'appointment_group': {
    'title': args.title,
    'participants_per_appointment': args.max_appointments,
    'max_appointments_per_participant': 1,
    'min_appointments_per_participant': 1,
    'publish': True,
  }
}

if args.description:
  params['appointment_group']['description'] = args.description
if args.location:
  params['appointment_group']['location_name'] = args.location

<<compute time slots>>
  
if not args.course:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, "An appointment group must belong to at least one "
                       "course; select the course(s) with -c/--course.")

course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)
for course in course_list:
  try:
    params['appointment_group']['context_codes'].append(f"course_{course.id}")
  except KeyError:
    params['appointment_group']['context_codes'] = [f"course_{course.id}"]

appointment_group = canvas.create_appointment_group(**params)
@

We get the duration from the [[args.time_slot_duration]] option.
Now we simply need to compute the time slots between the start and end times.

This is where an appointment group parts ways with a regular event.
We fill the interval by stepping from the start until the next slot no longer
fits, so the loop is bounded by the end time: without one there is no last
slot, and the list of appointments we have to send would never terminate.
An appointment group therefore cannot be open-ended the way a plain event can.

The end time and the slot duration are therefore preconditions rather than
options here, and we say so before computing anything.
We could instead let the arithmetic fail on the missing values, but [[end_time]]
is [[None]] here for exactly the reason the user cares about---they forgot a
flag---and a [[TypeError]] from deep inside [[arrow]] does not say which flag.
Naming the missing option is what turns a crash into an instruction.

The timeslots should be added to a \emph{dictionary} called
[[new_appointments]].
Although numbered by index, as a list, the API requires a dictionary with
string keys.

The bounds arrive here as instants, which is exactly what stepping through the
interval needs: the loop walks [[current_start]] forward by whole slot
durations and stops when the next slot would overshoot [[end]].
Each slot is formatted as it is emitted, because the pair of strings is what
Canvas stores---the arithmetic that produced them never sees text at all.
<<compute time slots>>=
if not args.end_time:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, "--end-time is required for appointment groups")
if not args.time_slot_duration:
  canvaslms.cli.err(1, "--time-slot-duration is required for appointment groups")

params["appointment_group"]["new_appointments"] = {}

slot_duration = datetime.timedelta(minutes=args.time_slot_duration)
current_start = start_time
end = end_time

slot_num = 0
while current_start + slot_duration <= end:
  slot = [
    utils.format_canvas_time(current_start),
    utils.format_canvas_time(current_start + slot_duration)
  ]
  params["appointment_group"]["new_appointments"][str(slot_num)] = slot
  current_start += slot_duration
  slot_num += 1
@


\section{Verifying the course guard on \texttt{calendar create}}

The broadcast failure is expensive precisely because it is silent:
a successful run prints nothing that would reveal that the event was
created seventeen times.
Tests are thus the only cheap way to keep the guard honest, so we pin
down the three cases that carry the decision---the default regex, an
explicit regex, and an appointment group with no course at all.

<<test [[calendar.py]]>>=
"""Tests for creating calendar events"""
import argparse
import copy

import arrow
import pytest

import canvaslms.cli.calendar as calendar

<<test helper functions>>
<<test functions>>
@

Recording the calls to [[create_calendar_event]] is not enough by
itself.
The command builds a single [[params]] dictionary and mutates its
[[context_code]] between calls, so a mock would hand us the same
dictionary several times and every recorded call would show the
\emph{last} course.
We therefore copy each call's arguments as they arrive.

<<test helper functions>>=
def record_created_events(canvas):
    """Make canvas record copies of create_calendar_event arguments"""
    created = []
    canvas.create_calendar_event.side_effect = \
        lambda **kwargs: created.append(copy.deepcopy(kwargs))
    return created

@

The command reads its arguments straight off [[args]], so we build the
namespace that [[argparse]] would have produced.
The defaults spell out the interesting case---no [[-c]] on the command
line, which argparse leaves as [[None]] now that the create parser
registers the option with [[default=None]]---and each test overrides
only what it varies.

