\section{Verifying that the ordering check reads instants}

The comparison the check used to make was right in the common case and wrong
in both directions outside it, and neither error shows up in an ordinary
session: minute-precision command lines format identically, so the mistake
needs sub-second times to surface at all.
That is precisely what a test can supply, and we use it to pin down each
direction---a well-formed event that alphabetical order rejects, and a
malformed one that it lets through.

<<test functions>>=
class TestCalendarCreateTimeOrdering:
    """Ordering must follow the instants, not the spelling of the timestamps"""

    <<time ordering test methods>>
@

Half a second is enough to spring the trap.
[[isoformat]] prints the fraction for the end time and omits it for the start,
so alphabetically the end sorts first even though it happens last---and the
string comparison rejected this event.
We compare the recorded timestamps as instants for the same reason the command
now does: the assertion should say that the event ends after it starts, not
that its two fields are spelled in a particular order.

<<time ordering test methods>>=
def test_subsecond_end_after_start_is_accepted(self, mock_canvas):
    """An end half a second after the start is a valid event"""
    mock_canvas.get_current_user.return_value.id = 42
    created = record_created_events(mock_canvas)

    calendar.calendar_create_command(
        {}, mock_canvas,
        create_args(start_time="2026-08-01 10:00:00",
                    end_time="2026-08-01 10:00:00.500000"))

    assert len(created) == 1
    event = created[0]["calendar_event"]
    assert arrow.get(event["end_at"]) > arrow.get(event["start_at"])

@

Exchanging the two times exposes the other direction.
The fraction now belongs to the start, so the end sorts last alphabetically
and the string comparison waved through an event that ends before it begins.
[[canvaslms.cli.err]] exits, so a rejection surfaces as [[SystemExit]] and
nothing reaches Canvas.

<<time ordering test methods>>=
def test_subsecond_end_before_start_is_rejected(self, mock_canvas):
    """An end half a second before the start must be rejected"""
    mock_canvas.get_current_user.return_value.id = 42

    with pytest.raises(SystemExit):
        calendar.calendar_create_command(
            {}, mock_canvas,
            create_args(start_time="2026-08-01 10:00:00.500000",
                        end_time="2026-08-01 10:00:00"))

    mock_canvas.create_calendar_event.assert_not_called()
